WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:02.800
Here’s something you’ve never, ever heard
before:

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:06.140
“Hey, good question, you should Bing it”

00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:08.870
Okay, maybe once.

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:12.389
But, except maybe as a punchline,
nobody uses Bing.

00:00:12.389 --> 00:00:15.660
Microsoft has tried, and tried,
and tried again,

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:19.980
They redesigned the website,
spent millions advertising a pun making Google

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:22.909
look better,
even paid people to use it.

00:00:22.909 --> 00:00:26.099
And yet, a decade later, Bing is still Bing.

00:00:26.099 --> 00:00:29.179
So, at this point, why not just abandon the
project?

00:00:29.179 --> 00:00:31.689
Well, because Bing isn’t actually a failure.

00:00:31.689 --> 00:00:32.689
Far from it.

00:00:32.689 --> 00:00:37.880
It’s a story of data, and control, and,
ultimately, the future of the internet.

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:44.570
In 2012, CEO Steve Ballmer announced Bing
was finally a real contender.

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:48.840
He said “By revealing our most popular searches,
we’re showing Bing is ready to compete with

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:49.840
the big boys.”

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:51.020
So what were they?

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:54.730
google, goggle, googlle, suicide, googler,
and hot sauce.

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:55.730
Hmmm…

00:00:55.730 --> 00:00:59.940
I mean, when it comes to spicy food, the crown
clearly goes to Bing.

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:03.340
Take, that, Google?

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:07.450
But, to be fair, we should’ve known Ballmer
was no prophet.

00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:12.159
After releasing the Windows Phone, you know,
that best-selling, world-changing sensation,

00:01:12.159 --> 00:01:17.330
he was so confident it would be successful
he held a funeral for the iPhone - literally,

00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:19.069
iPhones carried like caskets.

00:01:19.069 --> 00:01:20.069
Yeaaahhhh…

00:01:20.069 --> 00:01:22.759
So when I saw this chart of search engine
market share,

00:01:22.759 --> 00:01:26.200
I wiped my nonexistent glasses,
reloaded the page,

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:28.289
cleared the cache,
restarted my computer,

00:01:28.289 --> 00:01:30.310
threw it away,
then bought a new one.

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:34.600
But, somehow, it’s true:
A third of U.S. internet searches use Bing,

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:38.209
26% in the UK,
17% in Canada.

00:01:38.209 --> 00:01:40.959
And like Google, they sell ads to the highest
bidder.

00:01:40.959 --> 00:01:46.439
Multiply by over a billion users a month,
and Bing makes $5 billion a year.

00:01:46.439 --> 00:01:48.990
as in
one, two, three, four, five, six, seven, eight,

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:50.390
nine zeros.

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:53.219
Hundred dollar bills stacked higher than the
Burj Khalifa.

00:01:53.219 --> 00:01:54.350
Times five.

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:58.619
To put that in perspective, that’s what
YouTube, made, with basically no competitors,

00:01:58.619 --> 00:01:59.619
in 2016.

00:01:59.619 --> 00:02:02.840
The difference is Bing actually makes a profit.

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:07.389
Companies like Twitter and Snapchat lose money
for years, hoping money will come later.

00:02:07.389 --> 00:02:10.100
And yet, here’s Bing, actually in the black.

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:11.700
Hats off to you, Ballmer.

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:12.700
Oh wait

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:18.350
But seriously, how can this be? and what does
it mean?

00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:23.980
Microsoft has had plenty of ups and downs,
but one thing has always been consistent.

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:28.690
82% of the world’s computers run Windows,
and that hasn’t really changed.

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:33.420
Besides making a ton of money, this gives
them an incredibly powerful tool they’d

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:35.080
be dumb not to use.

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:40.100
In the same way a change to the iPhone is
a change to the entire industry, clear throat

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:44.790
Even the smallest adjustment to Windows has
a huge effect among hundreds of millions of

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:45.790
devices.

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:48.550
And that’s especially true for one simple
reason:

00:02:48.550 --> 00:02:50.500
People just don’t change their settings.

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:55.720
Microsoft lets you switch browsers and search
engines, but ignorance and apathy are on their

00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:56.720
side.

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:00.860
People don’t know how, or don’t care enough
to change them, making the defaults incredibly

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:01.910
popular.

00:03:01.910 --> 00:03:05.010
Internet Explorer may not be… universally
loved,

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:07.980
but it came with Windows, so it doesn’t
matter.

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:11.230
Same with Edge, their new browser,
which looks a little familiar…

00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:14.070
And they’ll do anything to get more users.

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:19.200
Microsoft and Apple may not be the best of
friends,

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:21.580
but the enemy of my enemy is my friend.

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:25.810
Nearly all of Google’s revenue comes from
search ads, most of which are mobile,

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:32.040
and even though only 12% are iOS devices,
they make up 75% of that mobile revenue.

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:36.140
Of course they’d prefer you generate that
money on Android, but in the meantime,

00:03:36.140 --> 00:03:39.290
it’s possible iOS users are worth more to
Google.

00:03:39.290 --> 00:03:42.070
They really benefit from and depend on Apple.

00:03:42.070 --> 00:03:43.820
But the feeling isn’t mutual.

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:45.730
Apple would like to keep a distance.

00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:48.540
To escape Google Maps, they built Apple Maps.

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:52.380
Which, wasn’t so good at keeping a distance

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:53.700
And that’s where Microsoft comes in.

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:58.580
In 2013, Siri waved goodbye to Google and
hello to Bing.

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:00.400
But money can heal all wounds…

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:05.350
Google now sends Apple a 3 billion dollar
check every year, and Apple says Fine,

00:04:05.350 --> 00:04:06.950
you can be our default search engine

00:04:06.950 --> 00:04:14.220
That’s maybe the world’s most expensive
flip of a switch.

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:18.239
The success of Bing isn’t really about Bing,
it’s about control.

00:04:18.239 --> 00:04:21.740
If people had to go out of their way to find
it, nobody would.

00:04:21.740 --> 00:04:26.310
The difference between a 5 billion dollar
business and a completely bankrupt one is

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:28.740
the power to decide what users see.

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:33.230
The decisions of tech companies will often
seem strange and reckless, like why pour so

00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:37.900
much money into the Windows and Fire Phones?
until you understand the larger goal: moving

00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:39.820
up the hierarchy of control.

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:43.420
The strategy of entire companies,
entire industries,

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:45.140
is to climb this triangle.

00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:49.250
The lower something is, the more companies
it relies on, and the greater its risk of

00:04:49.250 --> 00:04:50.250
being shut down.

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:54.190
At the very bottom are plugins, extensions,
mods, and hacks.

00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:58.320
They fundamentally depend on the obliviousness
or indifference of a larger company,

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:00.650
who they often mean very little to.

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:04.400
And when their goals differ, the larger company
always wins.

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:09.820
The Hackintosh community, for example, trick
macOS into running on their custom-built computers.

00:05:09.820 --> 00:05:14.290
It’s clever, but if Apple woke up on the
wrong side of the bed, they could end it faster

00:05:14.290 --> 00:05:17.320
than you can say How do you like them apples?

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:18.340
sigh

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:22.750
Slightly higher are websites, who can send
you any code they want, but are at the mercy

00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:24.580
of your browser to show it.

00:05:24.580 --> 00:05:28.360
And browsers, like all apps, are beholden
to the operating system, who may just say

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:29.860
Nah, I don’t think so

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:35.301
The company Astro makes an app to turn your
iPad into an extra computer monitor, and they

00:05:35.301 --> 00:05:39.070
wanted to add a button without covering the
screen, so they had a cool idea: They’d

00:05:39.070 --> 00:05:40.090
use the camera.

00:05:40.090 --> 00:05:43.330
You “press”, it detects less light, and
activates the button.

00:05:43.330 --> 00:05:48.220
A thumbs up from me, but a thumbs down from
Apple, who rejected it from the store.

00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:52.040
Or, they may really like your app,
incorporate it into the OS,

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:53.330
and kill your business.

00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:57.800
When Macs had a feature called Sherlock,
Someone made a companion called Watson, a

00:05:57.800 --> 00:05:59.800
$30 app with some extra features.

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:03.310
But it was so good that it came with the next
version of macOS.

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:08.180
And above all of these is the holy grail of
tech companies: physical devices.

00:06:08.180 --> 00:06:10.360
When you own the hardware, you own everything.

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:15.040
Jeff Bezos is the richest man in the world
not because he’s good at making profit,

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:19.290
and certainly not because he’s good at labeling
charts, but because he understands the power

00:06:19.290 --> 00:06:22.090
of getting Amazon in front of you, whatever
the cost.

00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:26.740
He would’ve happily lost money on the Fire
Phone just to get in more people’s hands.

00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:31.699
Kindle, Fire Tablets, Echo - they all make
little or no profit, but they’re incredibly

00:06:31.699 --> 00:06:32.699
important.

00:06:32.699 --> 00:06:36.330
It’s the same reason Windows Phone refused
to quit for so long.

00:06:36.330 --> 00:06:40.810
And you can bet companies like Facebook hate
being in the hands of phone companies.

00:06:40.810 --> 00:06:42.450
Please don’t do it, Mark.

00:06:42.450 --> 00:06:43.490
They’re gonna it

00:06:43.490 --> 00:06:47.669
Speaking of Facebook, the other reason control
is so important is data.

00:06:47.669 --> 00:06:51.949
More data means more and better ads,
and ads mean profit.

00:06:51.949 --> 00:06:56.010
Companies can buy it from places like Digi.me,
who you can sell your private information

00:06:56.010 --> 00:06:57.870
to for a few bucks,

00:06:57.870 --> 00:06:59.321
Or, companies can just get it themselves.

00:06:59.321 --> 00:07:02.699
And the higher on the triangle, the more they
can collect.

00:07:02.699 --> 00:07:10.520
It’s a terrible incentive that could forever
change the internet…

00:07:10.520 --> 00:07:13.750
In 2013, Facebook suddenly felt exceptionally
generous

00:07:13.750 --> 00:07:17.020
Hey, we should give everyone in the world
access to the internet

00:07:17.020 --> 00:07:19.980
So here’s what we’re going to do:
you get an internet,

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:22.699
you get an internet,
everyone gets an internet

00:07:22.699 --> 00:07:24.770
I know who I’m voting for in 2020…

00:07:24.770 --> 00:07:27.710
But it’s Facebook, so where’s the Black
Mirror twist?

00:07:27.710 --> 00:07:32.040
When users were surveyed, something strange
happened: more people said they used Facebook

00:07:32.040 --> 00:07:33.650
than used the Internet.

00:07:33.650 --> 00:07:40.360
65% of Nigerians and 61% of Indonesians they
asked agreed that “Facebook is the internet”

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:41.610
And that’s no accident…

00:07:41.610 --> 00:07:47.420
Make no mistake: what Facebook is so generously
donating is not the internet, but Facebook’s

00:07:47.420 --> 00:07:48.420
internet.

00:07:48.420 --> 00:07:52.900
They only give access to their website and
handful of others which meet their requirements.

00:07:52.900 --> 00:07:54.140
No net neutrality.

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:56.900
In other words, a clever way to get more users.

00:07:56.900 --> 00:08:01.320
Google is also getting into the philanthropy
business with what they call AMP.

00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:04.150
The technology is boring, so here’s a summary:

00:08:04.150 --> 00:08:07.699
What you see on a website is a fraction of
what’s actually there.

00:08:07.699 --> 00:08:10.110
the rest is, well, garbage.

00:08:10.110 --> 00:08:14.259
Garbage that tracks you for advertising, makes
the site look fancy, whatever.

00:08:14.259 --> 00:08:18.500
So Google says “All that garbage is really
slowing things down, why don’t we host your

00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:22.530
website for you, we’ll strip away the garbage
and it’ll load faster.”

00:08:22.530 --> 00:08:26.450
It sounds great, but, what do ya know, it
only works if you add to your website the

00:08:26.450 --> 00:08:28.820
same garbage it’s supposed to remove.

00:08:28.820 --> 00:08:31.860
Because it isn’t about speed so much as
control.

00:08:31.860 --> 00:08:34.310
Websites don’t need Google to remove their
garbage,

00:08:34.310 --> 00:08:37.229
if they want to load faster, they can just
do it themselves.

00:08:37.229 --> 00:08:40.320
Google wants you to hand them control of what
users see.

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:44.490
Sure, it’s “optional”, but if you say
no, they rank you lower in search results,

00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:45.589
so not really.

00:08:45.589 --> 00:08:49.850
Google and Facebook already have incredible
control over the internet, but they’d like

00:08:49.850 --> 00:08:50.850
all of it.

00:08:50.850 --> 00:08:54.100
Their dream isn’t to dominate the internet
but to be the internet.

00:08:54.100 --> 00:08:55.779
And that’s bad for everyone.

00:08:55.779 --> 00:09:00.000
The beauty of the internet is that power isn’t
in the hands of any one company.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:04.810
Anyone can do or learn anything without the
permission of a Google or a Facebook.

00:09:04.810 --> 00:09:09.690
That’s the power of a website like Brilliant.org,
you take control of your learning on topics

00:09:09.690 --> 00:09:12.140
like computer science, math, and physics.

00:09:12.140 --> 00:09:16.980
Behind all the technology in this video are
some really fascinating concepts like machine

00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:20.050
learning, neural networks, and computational
logic.

00:09:20.050 --> 00:09:24.620
All of which may sound intimidating, but are
actually pretty fun and rewarding to learn

00:09:24.620 --> 00:09:25.620
with Brilliant.

00:09:25.620 --> 00:09:30.570
In too many of my classes, and probably yours
too, we’re taught the steps to do a problem,

00:09:30.570 --> 00:09:32.500
but not really why, or how.

00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:36.820
That’s because we’re only memorizing one
specific problem, not actually understanding

00:09:36.820 --> 00:09:37.820
the process.

00:09:37.820 --> 00:09:41.100
The latter is what’s actually fun and useful
in the real world.

00:09:41.100 --> 00:09:45.130
And that’s what you get with Brilliant - you
practice the skills and see examples along

00:09:45.130 --> 00:09:46.130
the way.

00:09:46.130 --> 00:09:48.120
Here we’re learning about how computers
store information,

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:52.330
There’s an explanation, a visual, and a
chance to check our understanding.

00:09:52.330 --> 00:09:53.850
All very approachable.

00:09:53.850 --> 00:09:57.430
If we answer incorrectly, it shows us exactly
how to get the solution.

00:09:57.430 --> 00:10:02.040
It really is a fantastic way to learn, especially
if you’re currently a student or just like

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:03.180
learning new things.

